In this lesson we'll look at how to sketch a straight line from its equation. The big idea is simple: a linear equation in x and y always graphs as a straight line, and you only need two points to draw it. First up, the intercepts. These are your easiest points. To find the x-intercept, set y equal to zero and solve for x. To find the y-intercept, set x equal to zero and solve for y. If both intercepts exist, you've got your two points straight away. If one of them doesn't, just pick any two x values you like, work out the matching y, and plot those. Next, the slope-intercept form. This is when the equation is written as y equals m x plus c, where m is the gradient, or slope, of the line, and c is where the line crosses the y-axis. If your equation isn't already in that form, you can rearrange it so it is. Once you've got it, mark the y-intercept on the vertical axis, then use the gradient to step across to a second point. Finally, join those two points with a straight line and extend it across the page. Now let's see it in action.